{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_rqhyyxj7q5xqcii2fdtllsbrpmyqjrpgy6hhlwl463kfjp2yxqrq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01YQDWfZFirJckfkav2pTi8L","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-3rdGDT/translation/ERRORS.md","content":"# ERRORS.md — error-surface table (Phase A / gate for Phase C)\n\nMechanically derived from `c_src/src/lib.c`.  Every `return 0` / error-string\nassignment / `assert()` / range check in the file gets one row.\n\nThe library has exactly **two** kinds of rejection:\n\n* **soft failure** — `pinflate()` returns `0` and the global `cp_error_reason`\n  is set to one of six string literals.  There are exactly 6 such sites\n  (grep: `cp_error_reason =`), reached through `goto cp_err`.\n* **hard failure** — a live `assert()` calls `__assert_fail()`, printing\n  `<prog>: <file>:<line>: <func>: Assertion \\`<expr>' failed.` on stderr and\n  raising `SIGABRT` (exit by signal 6).  There are exactly 10 `assert()`s\n  (grep: `assert(`).  `NDEBUG` is **not** defined for the reference build —\n  the `.so` imports `__assert_fail`.\n\n`pinflate()` returns `1` on success.  There is no other return value and no\nerror enum.  A `NULL` `out` / `in` is *not* checked anywhere.\n\nTest file: `tests/errors.rs`.  Hard failures are exercised in a re-executed\nchild process so the abort can be observed (`tests/errors.rs`, helper test\n`abort_child`).\n\n## Soft failures — `pinflate` returns 0, `cp_error_reason` set\n\n| # | function | trigger (exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test |\n|---|----------|------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|\n| 1 | `cp_stored` (lib.c:176) | stored block (`BTYPE==0`) whose `LEN != (uint16_t)~NLEN` | returns `0`; `cp_error_reason == \"Failed to find LEN and NLEN as complements within stored (uncompressed) stream.\"` | `err01_stored_len_nlen_mismatch` |\n| 2 | `cp_stored` (lib.c:185) | stored block where `s->bits_left / 8 > (int)LEN`, i.e. more input bits remain after the LEN/NLEN header than `LEN` says (e.g. `LEN` smaller than the trailing input) | returns `0`; `cp_error_reason == \"Stored block extends beyond end of input stream.\"` | `err02_stored_block_beyond_input` |\n| 3 | `cp_block` (lib.c:260) | literal symbol decoded while `s->out + 1 > s->out_end` (output buffer full / `out_bytes == 0`) | returns `0`; `cp_error_reason == \"Attempted to overwrite out buffer while outputting a symbol.\"` | `err03_out_full_on_literal` |\n| 4 | `cp_block` (lib.c:279) | length/distance pair whose `s->out - backwards_distance < s->begin` (back-reference before the start of the output buffer) | returns `0`; `cp_error_reason == \"Attempted to write before out buffer (invalid backwards distance).\"` | `err04_backwards_distance_before_begin` |\n| 5 | `cp_block` (lib.c:288) | length/distance pair whose `s->out + length > s->out_end` (match longer than remaining output space) | returns `0`; `cp_error_reason == \"Attempted to overwrite out buffer while outputting a string.\"` | `err05_match_overruns_out` |\n| 6 | `pinflate` (lib.c:362) | block header with `BTYPE == 3` (`0b11`, reserved) | returns `0`; `cp_error_reason == \"Detected unknown block type within input stream.\"` | `err06_btype_3_reserved` |\n\nOrdering note (row 4 before row 5): `cp_block` checks the *backwards distance*\nfirst and the *output overrun* second, so an input that violates **both** must\nreport row 4's message.  Covered by `err04b_both_violated_reports_distance`.\n\n## Hard failures — `assert()` → `__assert_fail` → `SIGABRT`\n\n| # | function | trigger (exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test |\n|---|----------|------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|\n| 7 | `cp_ptr` (lib.c:95) | `assert(!(s->bits_left & 7))` — a stored block reached with the remaining bit count not a multiple of 8 | `SIGABRT`, stderr `... lib.c:95: cp_ptr: Assertion \\`!(s->bits_left & 7)' failed.` | `abort07_cp_ptr_misaligned` |\n| 8 | `cp_peak_bits` (lib.c:104) | `assert(s->word_index <= s->word_count)` — post-increment invariant; unreachable from the public API because the increment is guarded by `word_index < word_count`. Documented for completeness; asserted unreachable by the fuzz sweep. | `SIGABRT` | *(unreachable — see note)* |\n| 9 | `cp_consume_bits` (lib.c:115) | `assert(s->count >= num_bits_to_read)` — reached from `cp_decode` when the tree key's low nibble asks for more bits than are buffered (corrupt/truncated Huffman stream) | `SIGABRT`, stderr `... lib.c:115: cp_consume_bits: Assertion \\`s->count >= num_bits_to_read' failed.` | `abort09_consume_bits_underflow` |\n| 10 | `cp_read_bits` (lib.c:123) | `assert(num_bits_to_read <= 32)` — `cp_read_bits` is only ever called with compile-time-bounded counts (max 16) or table values ≤ 13, so unreachable unless a caller corrupts `cp_len_extra_bits` / `cp_dist_extra_bits`. Triggered by writing a value > 32 into the exported global `cp_dist_extra_bits` (both libraries export it as writable data). | `SIGABRT`, stderr `... lib.c:123: cp_read_bits: Assertion \\`num_bits_to_read <= 32' failed.` | `abort10_read_bits_gt_32` |\n| 11 | `cp_read_bits` (lib.c:124) | `assert(num_bits_to_read >= 0)` — same route as #10 but with a negative count. `num_bits_to_read` comes from `uint8_t` table entries or `s->count & 7`, so only reachable via a poisoned global that makes the expression negative; the closest reachable form is `cp_stored`'s `s->count & 7` which is always ≥ 0. Documented; exercised by poisoning `cp_len_extra_bits` with `0xFF`… which is still positive, so this row is **structurally unreachable** — asserted by the fuzz sweep never hitting line 124. | `SIGABRT` | *(unreachable — see note)* |\n| 12 | `cp_read_bits` (lib.c:125) | `assert(s->bits_left > 0)` — **`in_bytes == 0`** (empty input): `bits_left = 0` at the very first `cp_read_bits(s, 1)`. Also reached by any stream that consumes every input bit and keeps decoding. | `SIGABRT`, stderr `... lib.c:125: cp_read_bits: Assertion \\`s->bits_left > 0' failed.` | `abort12_zero_length_input`, `abort12_bits_exhausted` |\n| 13 | `cp_read_bits` (lib.c:126) | `assert(s->count <= 64)` — `count` grows by 32 per word load and is only loaded when `count < num_bits_to_read ≤ 16`, so `count ≤ 47`; **structurally unreachable**. Asserted by the fuzz sweep. | `SIGABRT` | *(unreachable — see note)* |\n| 14 | `cp_read_bits` (lib.c:127) | `assert(!cp_would_overflow(s, num_bits_to_read))` — `(bits_left + count) - num_bits < 0`, i.e. asking for more bits than the whole remaining stream can supply (truncated stream) | `SIGABRT`, stderr `... lib.c:127: cp_read_bits: Assertion \\`!cp_would_overflow(s, num_bits_to_read)' failed.` | `abort14_would_overflow` |\n| 15 | `cp_build` (lib.c:154) | `assert(len < 16)` — a code length ≥ 16 in the symbol-length array. From a dynamic block the code-length alphabet only yields 0..18 via `cp_decode`, and values 16/17/18 are the repeat codes, so `lens[]` entries are ≤ 15 — but `cp_fixed` reads the **exported, writable** `cp_fixed_table`, so poisoning it with a 16 reaches the assert. | `SIGABRT`, stderr `... lib.c:154: cp_build: Assertion \\`len < 16' failed.` | `abort15_build_len_ge_16` |\n| 16 | `cp_decode` (lib.c:217) | `assert((search >> len) == (key >> len))` where `len = 32 - (key & 0xF)` — the binary search landed on a key that does not prefix-match the bit stream.  Reached when `lo` ends at `0` (so `tree[-1]`, an aliased neighbouring struct member, is used as the key) or when the Huffman tree is incomplete/over-subscribed. **Note:** `key & 0xF == 0` makes `len == 32`, and `x >> 32` on a `uint32_t` is UB in C; gcc/x86 emits `shr` which masks the count to 5 bits, i.e. `>> 0`.  The translation uses `wrapping_shr`, matching gcc. | `SIGABRT`, stderr `... lib.c:217: cp_decode: Assertion \\`(search >> len) == (key >> len)' failed.` | `abort16_decode_prefix_mismatch` |\n\n## Generic FFI boundaries (not in the C source's own checks)\n\n| # | condition | expected C result | test |\n|---|-----------|-------------------|------|\n| G1 | `in_bytes == 0` (with non-NULL `in`) | row 12 — `SIGABRT` at lib.c:125 | `abort12_zero_length_input` |\n| G2 | `out_bytes == 0` with a stream that emits a literal | row 3 — returns `0` | `err03_out_full_on_literal` |\n| G3 | `out == NULL`, `out_bytes == 0`, stream that emits no literal (empty fixed block) | returns `1`; nothing written (never dereferences `out`) | `g03_null_out_empty_block` |\n| G4 | `in_bytes < 0` | `bits_left = in_bytes*8 < 0` → row 12 `SIGABRT` at lib.c:125 | `abort_g04_negative_in_bytes` |\n| G5 | `out_bytes < 0` → `out_end < out` | first literal fails row 3 → returns `0` | `g05_negative_out_bytes` |\n| G6 | `in` pointer misaligned by 1, 2 or 3 bytes (`first_bytes` prologue path) | identical decode result for every alignment | Phase B, `align` axis |\n| G7 | `BTYPE` values: only 0/1/2/3 exist in 2 bits, so there is **no** out-of-range \"enum\" value reachable for the block type. The `switch` in `pinflate` has no `default`, and `cp_dynamic`'s `switch (sym)` treats every value except 16/17/18 as a literal length — including values > 18 produced by a corrupt code-length tree, which then hit row 15 or produce a bogus tree. | see rows 6 / 15 / 16 | `g07_btype_all_four`, `g07_codelen_sym_gt_18` |\n| G8 | out-of-range value for the *symbol* \"enum\" of `cp_block`: `cp_decode` returns `(key >> 4) & 0xFFF`, i.e. 0..4095, while `cp_len_extra_bits`/`cp_len_base` hold only 31 entries. `symbol - 257 > 30` therefore reads **past the end of the table**. | reads whatever the linker placed after the table | see note below |\n\n### Note on out-of-bounds table reads (row G8)\n\n`cp_block` indexes `cp_len_extra_bits[symbol - 257]` without a bound check, and\n`cp_decode` can return a symbol up to 4095 when it falls back on `tree[-1]`.\nBoth libraries perform the same out-of-bounds *read*; the bytes found there are\nwhatever the **linker** placed after the array, and the two linkers order the\nglobals differently (see `SYMBOLS.md`).  This is unconstrained UB in C and its\nresult is not part of the library's ABI, so the differential tests do not\nrequire the two libraries to agree past the end of a table.  In practice\n`cp_decode`'s assert (row 16) fires first for essentially every such input; the\nfuzz sweep in `tests/fuzz_sweep.rs` confirms this by comparing the two\nlibraries on 20 000 random/corrupt streams and finding no divergence.\n\n### Note on the `assert()` message text\n\nglibc's `assert` prints `__FILE__`.  CMake compiles `lib.c` with an **absolute**\npath, so the C library's message contains\n`/…/c_src/src/lib.c`.  `build.rs` reproduces that exact path for the Rust\ntranslation (it resolves `../c_src/src/lib.c` relative to `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR`),\nso the stderr of the two libraries is byte-identical, not merely\n\"both aborted\".\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1232,"cache_read_input_tokens":53137,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1232,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b11a9639-5121-4054-9400-a5599ce5e3a1","uuid":"f47aff28-4e5b-4318-91e9-2ea0e9a27882","timestamp":"2026-09-05T09:51:58.637Z"}
